  "query_preview": "(Today through Thursday)...\nIssued at 306 AM EDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nToday through Saturday night... \n\nSurface high pressure remaining the dominant weather feature will \ncontinue to promote mostly quiet weather conditions. Look for \nincreasing mid-high clouds as an upper wave moves through later \ntoday. No precipitation is expected from the passing wave due to \nsubsidence induced dry air in the lower troposphere. Deep mixing \nwill likely drop dewpoints lower than NBM suggest so high resolution \nguidance was incorporated.\n\nAnother impulse will move through the region on Saturday. Guidance \nsuggest subsidence induced dry air from the surface high should \nlimit precipitation chances so the forecast remains mostly dry. That \nsaid, weak forcing and deeper moisture advecting into central \nIndiana may support isolated showers. Look for temperatures to \nremain cooler than normal with highs generally in the mid 70s to low \n80s today with highs in the low 80s Saturday.  \n\nSunday into next week...\n\nModel guidance depicts an amplified upper ridge centered near the \nRockies with northwesterly flow across the Great Lakes Region during \nthe latter half of the period. This should allow for a few \nshortwaves to pass through the region late this weekend into next \nweek. These disturbances along with deeper moisture advecting into \ncentral Indiana will return chances for convection to the \nforecast at times late Sunday into next week. Weak surface high \npressure and an EML advecting towards the region within \nsouthwesterly should keep weather conditions mostly quiet through \nmuch of the day Sunday.\n\nDiverging model solutions leads to lower confidence in exact details \nduring this period, but ensemble guidance suggest that at least \nmoderate-strong instability will be in place each day. This along \nwith enhancement of mid-upper level flow from the passing shortwaves \ncould support the threat for severe weather, mainly Sunday night \nthrough Monday. However, this will depend on how far east the upper \nridge builds and where the shortwaves aloft track. Uncertainty will \nremain high until models become better aligned. \n\nWarm air advection ahead of the aforementioned disturbances should \nwarm temperatures well into the 80s to lower 90s Sunday through \nTuesday. Dewpoints returning to the low-mid 70s will likely lead to \npeak heat indices around or a few degrees above 100F on Monday.",
